# Cursor Rules — Rule Harness Repo

## Commit Discipline
- Commit after every unit of change — one logical change, one commit.
- Use conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`
- Write *why* in the commit body, not just what.

## SOLID
- Single Responsibility: `engine.ts` checks rules, `agent.ts` runs the loop, checkers are pure functions. Keep each file doing exactly one thing.
- Open/Closed: Add new rule types by adding new files + extending the union type. Don't modify existing checkers to handle new cases.
- Liskov: All rule checkers must satisfy `(rule, action) => RuleViolation | null`. No exceptions.
- Interface Segregation: Rule-type-specific fields belong on the specific interface, not on `BaseRule`.
- Dependency Inversion: `RuleEngine` dispatches over `Rule[]`. Don't let checkers know about other checkers.

## KISS
- Prefer a plain function over a class. Prefer a `switch` over a registry. Prefer flat over nested.
- No abstraction layers unless they remove real, existing duplication.
- If a change adds more lines than it removes complexity, question it.

## Clean Code
- No `any` without a comment explaining why it can't be avoided.
- No silent exception swallowing.
- Functions under ~40 lines. Max 2 levels of nesting before extracting.
- Name for what something IS, not what it DOES: `RuleViolation`, not `CheckOutput`.

## File Responsibilities (do not cross these lines)
- `src/rules/*.ts` — pure functions, no I/O, no side effects
- `src/engine.ts` — dispatch + retry budget only
- `src/agent.ts` — tool-use loop, file I/O, subprocess calls
- `src/types.ts` — types only, zero logic
- `src/cli.ts` — arg parsing + wiring only

## Language/Runtime
- TypeScript strict mode is on. Fix type errors, don't cast around them.
- ESM (`"type": "module"`). Use `.js` extensions in imports.
- No `process.exit()` outside `cli.ts`.
